==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|parlour}} {{qualifier|British}}

===Etymology===
{{inh+|en|enm|parlour}}, from {{der|en|fro|parleor}}, {{m|fro|parloir}}, {{m|fro|parleoir}}, from the verb {{m|fro|parler||to speak}}; compare {{der|en|ML.|parlātōrium}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈpɑːlə/}}
* {{a|GA}} {{enPR|pärʹlər}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈpɑɹlɚ/}}
* {{a|Nigeria}} {{IPA|en|/ˈpaːlɔː/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-parlour.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɑː(ɹ)lə(ɹ)|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|dated|except in|Mid-Atlantic US}} The [[living room]] of a [[house]], or a room for entertaining guests; a room for talking; a [[sitting-room]] or [[drawing room]].
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=12|passage=So, after a spell, he decided to make the best of it and shoved us into the front '''parlor'''. 'Twas a dismal sort of place, with hair wreaths, and wax fruit, and tin lambrekins, and land knows what all.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} The apartment in a monastery or nunnery where the residents are permitted to meet and converse with each other or with visitors from the outside.
# {{lb|en|archaic}} A [[comfortable]] [[room]] in a [[public house]].
# {{lb|en|chiefly|Southern US}} A covered open-air [[patio]].
# A [[shop]] or other [[business]] selling goods or services specified by context.
#: {{ux|en|a tattoo '''parlor'''}}
# A [[shed]] used for [[milking]] [[cattle]].
# {{lb|en|Philippines}} {{short for|en|beauty parlor}}

